{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And the priest who offers the burnt offering of a man</b> — This ruling applies to all offerings. Scripture states 'burnt offering,' and the same law applies to sin offering and guilt offering mentioned above, but the slaughter-offerings of peace offerings are not so. Therefore Scripture mentioned the law of the priests in the middle of the offerings, before it speaks of the peace offerings.\n\nIts midrash in Torat Kohanim (chapter 9, section 2): 'I have only the burnt offering; whence do I derive the hides of most holy things? Scripture teaches: \"that he offered\" (אֲשֶׁר הִקְרִיב). One might think I include the hides of lesser holy things — Scripture teaches: \"burnt offering\" (עֹלָה). Just as the burnt offering is uniquely most holy, so the lesser holy things are excluded.'\n\nAccording to the plain sense, there was no need to say this regarding the sin offering and guilt offering, for they are priestly gifts and the priests acquire the flesh and acquire the hide. But regarding the burnt offering, it was necessary to say that they acquire the hide. And this is the midrash of Rabbi [Yehudah], who said: 'We in our entirety had no need except for the hide of the burnt offering, since everywhere the hide follows the flesh' — in Torat Kohanim (chapter 9, section 4) and in Zevaḥim (103a)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:13.651923747Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}